# tools/t/tutorial_output_witness.rish -- a tutorial's quoted output is run back against the
# command above it.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/t/tutorial_output_witness.rish
#
# WHY. A tutorial's whole promise is here is what you will see, and nothing in this tree read one.
# qa_report_card scores Truth by whether cited PATHS resolve; document_mirror_scan proves two homes
# hold identical bytes; a fenced output block holds neither a path nor a mirror. So
# docs-geode/tutorials/the-first-hour.md quoted a three-line ending for its toolchain fetch where
# the command prints four, and the page read Truth 100 the whole time it was wrong
# (20260909.174000). The repair that lap was one page; this is the general form.
#
# WHAT IS GATED, hard, at zero: drift -- a pair whose command ran and printed something other than
# what the page quotes.
#
# WHAT RUNS. Only a fence whose every line is one plain invocation of rishi/bin/rishi or sh naming
# a TRACKED script, with no redirect, pipe, semicolon, ampersand, backtick, dollar or glob. The
# test is on what EXECUTES rather than on what it is handed, so the resolver in the demos page
# keeps its deliberately stale argument and the pair still checks.
#
# WHAT IS HELD, named rather than run: a fence that makes a directory, writes a file, or names a
# script the reader is about to write. Held is a ratchet under a ceiling that only falls.
#
# A BLOCK THAT HONESTLY MOVES DECLARES ITSELF, with a reason, on the line above its fence:
# <!-- volatile: why it moves -->. It reports and never gates. A reasonless declaration buys
# nothing, which the pen proves.
#
# THE ROOM WRITES A SECOND CLAIM SHAPE, and this read it as nothing for one day (20260910). A page
# may quote SOME of a longer report -- "Two lines from the full output:" -- and that sentence sits
# between the command and the block, which the parser used to treat as the end of the candidate.
# Measured over the same 38 pages: nine pairs, and the guard saw six. A selection declares itself
# the same way a volatile block does, with a reason:
#
#     <!-- selected: two lines of a longer report -->
#
# and is checked by CONTAINMENT IN ORDER -- every quoted line appears in what ran, in the order the
# page prints them. Order is part of the claim: a page listing a verdict above the count that
# produced it teaches the output's shape wrongly even when both lines are present, and the pen
# proves that leg alone by removing the order strand and watching it go red.
#
# AND CONTAINMENT IN ORDER SAYS NOTHING ABOUT WHAT STANDS BETWEEN (20260910). A page may quote four
# lines that all print, in the order it prints them, and pass over a refusal standing in the middle
# of the run -- and every reading above calls that clean. So a selection is read a second time for
# its SHAPE: one unbroken run of the real output, or a gathering of lines that stand apart. The
# test tries every start position in what ran, so the answer is outright rather than an alignment
# somebody chose. Measured over the same nine pairs: the fascia pair in the demos room quotes two
# adjacent lines and reads contiguous; the announced-length pair beside it quotes one `met:` line
# and three summary lines with FOUR further `met:` lines standing between them, and the scan now
# names all four.
#
# `selected_scattered` is a CEILING rather than a zero, since gathering is what a selection is for.
# It falls on repair and rises only by a hand adding a declared selection, an edit standing in the
# same commit as the page. What it refuses in silence is the case it exists for: a selection that
# reads as one run today with a line wedged into the middle of it tomorrow. A ceiling on the COUNT
# of skipped lines was declined and the reason is measurable -- `announced_length_scan` prints one
# more line for every ladder anybody announces, so that ceiling would red on a page nobody touched.
# The pen proves the distinction from both ends: a wedged line bites, and honest growth between the
# quoted lines goes free with the shape count unmoved.
#
# PROSE WITH NOTHING DECLARING IT is counted as undeclared_after_prose and named, never checked and
# never gated. That is the honest half: the page may be quoting a subset, reattributing the output
# to another invocation, or setting two unrelated blocks side by side, and those want three
# different tests. The first hour holds a real one -- its "run it twice" block belongs to a
# DIFFERENT command, so pairing it would invent a claim the writer never made. What changed is that
# the tree can now see it, since a block nothing reads and a block nothing MAY read read alike from
# outside and only one of them is a gap.
#
# HOW FAR A BLOCK MAY SIT from its command is bounded at twelve lines, about a screen of prose, and
# the pen proves the bound from both sides.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH. Stdout alone -- two interleaved streams give an order that depends on
# buffering rather than on the program. Only the pairs the docs-geode room already writes. And it
# proves what a command PRINTS, never that the command is the right one to teach.
#
#
# NOT tools/d/docs_command_path_witness.rish, which stands beside it in the same room and does a
# different job: that one proves every path printed inside a fenced block RESOLVES for a reader who
# clones, after a folded room left two pages naming a file at its elder address. This one proves
# what a command PRINTS. A page can name paths that all resolve and still promise output no command
# produces -- which is exactly what the first hour did. Two jobs, two names.
# Pen: tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_control.sh -- forty-three behaviors on real git
# repositories in a throwaway pen, every refusal planted and then lifted, the gap bound and the
# scattered ceiling each shown from both sides, the order strand proven load-bearing by a mutation
# that reds one leg alone, and the shape strand by one that reds seven.
#
# This header read "twenty-nine" until 20260910, and the pen emitted THIRTY-ONE the whole time. The
# number had been taken from the witness's own assertion list rather than from the control's
# output, so it counted the reader instead of the thing read -- which is the same fault one room
# over as a page quoting output nobody ran. Both rosters agree at forty-three now, and the count
# below is read off the control rather than off this file.
# Run from the repository root.

say "tutorial-output: Language EN -- a quoted output block is a claim about behavior, and a claim nobody checks is a claim that decays."
say "tutorial-output: Style Gauge, Meter -- the reading runs the commands, so anyone may check the number by running them too."
say "tutorial-output: Lens Aether -- a guard that cannot red guards nothing, so every refusal here is planted in a pen and watched to bite."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "tutorial-output: a page quotes output its command no longer prints -- read the scan's own detail lines"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=every_quoted_block_still_prints" else "tutorial-output: the scan must reach the clean verdict"
assert scan.out contains "drift=0" else "tutorial-output: no page may quote output its command does not print"
assert (scan.out contains "verdict=a_selection_broke_its_run" == false) else "tutorial-output: a selection quoted as one run must still be one run"

# A shape reading that never fires reads exactly like a corpus with no selections in it, so both
# counts are asserted present rather than only the refusal being absent.
assert scan.out contains "selected_contiguous=" else "tutorial-output: the shape of every selection must be read, not only its containment"
assert scan.out contains "selected_scattered=" else "tutorial-output: a gathering must be counted where a reader can see it"

# A meter reading an empty corpus reports clean while proving nothing, so the corpus is measured.
assert (scan.out contains "pairs=0" == false) else "tutorial-output: a corpus with no pairs would report clean while reading nothing at all"
assert (scan.out contains "checked=0" == false) else "tutorial-output: every pair held and none run is a guard that ran no command"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_control.sh"]
say control.out
assert control.ok else "tutorial-output: the control must complete"
assert control.out contains "case=match_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a page quoting exactly what its command prints must pass"
assert control.out contains "case=match_counted_exact ok" else "tutorial-output: a matching pair must be counted exact, which is what proves the command ran"
assert control.out contains "case=drift_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: a quoted line the command never prints must be refused"
assert control.out contains "case=drift_named ok" else "tutorial-output: a refusal must name the page it refused"
assert control.out contains "case=drift_measured ok" else "tutorial-output: a refusal must say how far apart the quote and the output are"
assert control.out contains "case=drift_lifted ok" else "tutorial-output: a refusal that survives its own repair is one nobody can act on"
assert control.out contains "case=volatile_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a declared moving block must report rather than gate"
assert control.out contains "case=volatile_counted ok" else "tutorial-output: a declared pair must be counted volatile"
assert control.out contains "case=volatile_not_drift ok" else "tutorial-output: a declared pair must leave the drift count alone"
assert control.out contains "case=reasonless_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: a declaration with no reason must excuse nothing"
assert control.out contains "case=redirect_held ok" else "tutorial-output: a fence that writes a file must be held"
assert control.out contains "case=redirect_not_run ok" else "tutorial-output: a fence outside the roster must run nothing"
assert control.out contains "case=untracked_held ok" else "tutorial-output: a script the reader writes must be held rather than called a drift"
assert control.out contains "case=ceiling_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: the held ceiling must refuse one pair past it"
assert control.out contains "case=ceiling_met_free ok" else "tutorial-output: the held ceiling must pass at exactly its own number"
assert control.out contains "case=prose_between_named ok" else "tutorial-output: prose between the blocks must be counted and named rather than dropped in silence"
assert control.out contains "case=prose_between_unchecked ok" else "tutorial-output: an undeclared block behind prose must not be checked, since the prose may reattribute it"
assert control.out contains "case=prose_between_ungated ok" else "tutorial-output: an undeclared block behind prose must report rather than gate"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_counted ok" else "tutorial-output: a declared selection must be counted selected"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a true selection of a longer report must pass"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_drift_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: a selection quoting a line no command prints must be refused"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_drift_named ok" else "tutorial-output: a refused selection must name the line that never printed"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_order_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: a selection whose lines all print in the wrong order must be refused -- order is part of the claim"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_lifted ok" else "tutorial-output: a repaired selection must go free again"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_reasonless_declares_nothing ok" else "tutorial-output: a selection with no reason must declare nothing"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_run_contiguous ok" else "tutorial-output: a selection quoted as one unbroken run must read contiguous"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_run_not_scattered ok" else "tutorial-output: an unbroken run must not be counted as a gathering"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_gathered_counted ok" else "tutorial-output: a selection gathered from lines that stand apart must be counted"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_gathered_named ok" else "tutorial-output: the line standing between the quoted ones must be named, or a reader cannot weigh what the page passes over"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_gathered_not_drift ok" else "tutorial-output: a lawful gathering must never be called a drift"
assert control.out contains "case=scattered_ceiling_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: one gathering past the ceiling must refuse"
assert control.out contains "case=scattered_ceiling_names_the_middle ok" else "tutorial-output: a refusal must say what the page passes over"
assert control.out contains "case=scattered_ceiling_met_free ok" else "tutorial-output: the scattered ceiling must pass at exactly its own number"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_break_bitten ok" else "tutorial-output: a run broken by a line wedged into its middle must refuse -- the page teaches a run that no longer exists"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_break_names_the_wedge ok" else "tutorial-output: a broken run must name the line that broke it"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_growth_keeps_shape ok" else "tutorial-output: honest growth between quoted lines must leave the shape count where it stood"
assert control.out contains "case=selection_growth_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a report that prints one more line must never red a page nobody touched"
assert control.out contains "case=gap_at_bound_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a block exactly at the gap bound must still pair"
assert control.out contains "case=gap_past_bound_dropped ok" else "tutorial-output: a block past the gap bound must not pair, since a reader has stopped holding the command"
assert control.out contains "case=lone_command_free ok" else "tutorial-output: a command promising no output must be counted as no claim"
assert control.out contains "case=empty_corpus_refused ok" else "tutorial-output: a corpus with no pairs must refuse rather than read clean"
assert control.out contains "case=no_tree_told_apart ok" else "tutorial-output: a directory with no git tree must read distinctly"
assert control.out contains "case=list_names_pairs ok" else "tutorial-output: the list verb must name every pair with its verdict"
assert control.out contains "control=ok" else "tutorial-output: the control must agree with itself"

say ""
say "GREEN: tutorial-output -- every quoted output block in docs-geode still prints what its page promises, whole or as a declared selection whose shape is read as well as its contents, with forty-three behaviors proven in a pen and every refusal bitten and then lifted."
